SM(599,{"n":"Hebrews 8.1-13","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Hebrews 8.1-13.mp3","d":2446.2,"s":[[0.32,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in biblical studies, and it's an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[19.6,"Okay, we'll go ahead and get started."],[21.28,"And when we get started here in just a moment, we will pick up uh at the beginning of St."],[28.64,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews, uh, chapter eight."],[34.0,"Um, and so last time, I'm definitely not going to reiterate everything from last time, but um, last time uh we were focused on in chapter seven, we sort of finally, after uh uh St."],[49.92,"Paul had digressed a little, gotten back to talking about Melchizedek."],[55.44,"That was the thrust."],[57.44,"And talking about connecting. the priesthood of Melchizedek in Genesis and in Psalm 110, which St"],[66.76,"Paul's Epistle to the Hebrews is really sort of a homily on, connecting that to Christ's priesthood, and then contrasting that with the Levitical priesthood of the Torah."],[85.9,"Which was a particular priesthood for a particular window of time."],[90.4,"And the Levitical priesthood obviously did not precede Levi."],[94.8,"It didn't even precede Moses."],[96.33,"Until you get the Torah, there is no Levitical priesthood."],[100.41,"And then it also has an end, as he's pointing out."],[103.97,"Whereas this priesthood of Melchizedek, as it's spoken of in Psalm 110, and as we see in the story of Melchizedek himself, is something that supersedes that."],[117.25,"And so that's why Christ's priesthood supersedes the Levitical priesthood of the old covenant."],[125.17,"And Psalm 110, by referring to it as his priesthood as being a priesthood after the order of Melchizedek, was saying that Christ's priesthood was going to supersede it."],[137.89,"And by supersede, that doesn't mean that's the old thing, and this replaces it."],[143.73,"It means that Christ's priesthood does what that priesthood did, but does it fully and completely."],[148.29,"but Whereas the Levitical priesthood was doing that same thing doing it limitedly and provisionally."],[158.27,"And so now that the fullness is here, we don't need sort of the partial."],[163.49,"Was the idea."],[164.85,"And so uh tonight, as we move on, uh St."],[169.49,"Paul's gonna kind of segue from the priesthood proper and move to more of the liturgical aspect of the priesthood, what the priest does."],[181.22,"In terms of both the uh the tabernacle structure and in terms of sacrifices, sacrificial ritual."],[192.38,"So that's sort of where now we're going to move is from the priesthood into that."],[197.12,"Because if we first had in the in the first few chapters, it was about Christ as son of God, as the Messiah."],[206.31,"As as the king, as the Lord, and now Christ is the high priest, so we talked about what he does qua king and lord."],[216.79,"Now we're talking about what Christ does qua high priest."],[221.03,"And that's that's what we're in sort of the midst of right now."],[224.55,"Uh so that said, unless anybody has any questions or comments or witty anecdotes, or we shared a few of those earlier."],[234.71,"I think we've we' were we''rere spent on those."],[235.59,"we Uh'll go ahead and pick up in St."],[235.67,"'re Pauls Epistle to the Hebws, chapter 8, verse 1."],[245.07,"Now, this is the main point of the things we are saying."],[248.99,"We have such a high priest who is seated at the right hand of the throne of the majesty in the heavens, a minister of the sanctuary and of the true tabernacle which the Lord erected, and not man."],[263.15,"Okay. So uh now he says, now this is the main point of the things we are saying."],[270.35,"He doesn't mean this is the main point of the whole epistle."],[274.83,"It's one of them, but he's saying that he's he's sort of saying, you know, okay, so now we've talked about all this stuff with Melchized."],[282.05,"And Levitical priesthood, Belchized priesthood."],[285.49,"Here's the point of all that."],[287.19,"The point of all that is that of describing the superiority of this priesthood of the Order of Melchizedek is that we have a such a high priest."],[297.05,"And it's obviously obvious who he's talking about because he's already talked about Christ being seated at the right hand of the throne of the majesty in the heavens."],[306.58,"That's Psalm 110, verse 1."],[308.1,"That's where he started."],[310.02,"Yahweh said to my Lord, sit at my right hand until I make your enemies your footstool."],[315.06,"And so what he adds then is what's there in verse 2"],[322.52,"So the the fact that Christ is the high priest who is there at the right hand of Father also means that he is a minister of the sanctuary."],[335.06,"The holy place."],[336.87,"Because the place where God is, is the holy place."],[342.2,"And of the true tabernacle, which the Lord erected, and not man."],[347.08,"Okay. So a couple of points here."],[349.48,"Notice, and and we're going to notice this throughout that uh all through here, he talks about the tabernacle, not the temple."],[362.0,"Um, and and this is something important to notice because although Christ the apostles, while they're in Jerusalem and while it's there, including St."],[374.6,"Paul himself, go to pray at the temple in Jerusalem and uh participate in other ritual acts of worship at the temple in Jerusalem, uh the temple is actually a much more ambivalent category in Scripture, not just in the New Testament, in Scripture, than we often give it credit for being."],[399.96,"And part of that is because we're living in the United States, and so under the influence of evangelicalism and the Schofielderence Ref Bible and dispensational theology, uh, the idea of some kind of rebuilt temple in Jerusalem as this eschatological promise."],[421.83,"This good good thing that's gonna happen."],[423.83,"So the church brothers talk about it happening as a very bad thing, but uh but the idea that this is a good thing, that's kind of in the theological atmosphere around us."],[435.69,"And it's a kind of affected uh how we read the Bible."],[439.61,"But if you go back, I mean going back to Solomon."],[445.74,"Um, Solomon um when he goes to dedicate the temple, um, first of all, the the with the tabernacle, I mean the tabernacle's there in the Torah."],[461.86,"Nowhere in the Torah does it say, oh, and when you get to the land, build a permanent temple."],[469.7,"Does say that."],[471.3,"And everything in the tabernacle, from the curtains to the poles, to all of the pieces of furniture, to exacting detail, are given twice in the Torah."],[484.27,"It makes it makes the end of the book of Exodus a real bore to read."],[487.89,"Because, you know, you could make it through once through the three cubits long and two cubits high and two cubits wide."],[494.67,"And all the all those things."],[496.27,"But then after the instructions are given, it then repeats it all, saying, and so they built it."],[503.03,"Three qubits long and two qubits high and two qubits wide."],[506.31,"The exact same information."],[507.51,"The point being, everything was done exactly as it was commanded."],[510.55,"You don't have any narrative like that surrounding the temple."],[516.45,"David wants to build it."],[518.05,"God tells him, No."],[520.55,"You're not going to."],[522.87,"Solomon builds it."],[524.15,"And then Solomon prays this prayer of dedication, where basically he talks about the temple of Jerusalem the way the pagans talked about their temples."],[533.27,"Where he basically says to God, Oh, grant whatever we pray toward this temple."],[539.12,"And when we make vows and offer sacrifices at this temple, honor them, and basically do what we want you to do when we do stuff at this temple."],[548.28,"And God's response is given."],[551.4,"And God's response is thick with he doesn't live in buildings made by human hands."],[559.48,"And he doesn't, and he's not constrained to it, and you he's not tied to it."],[565.56,"But he will sort of deign to right uh honor sort of the effort."],[573.18,"And I will be present with you here, but not on human terms."],[580.0,"And so we see he leaves and Nebuchadnezzar destroys it."],[586.14,"And at the time when you read Jeremiah, everybody in Jerusalem is saying there's no way the Babylonians can take Jerusalem because they would destroy the temple."],[596.7,"And this is God's temple."],[597.9,"He's not going to let his temple be destroyed."],[601.47,"So the way they looked at the temple and the way God treated the temple were very different."],[607.68,"And if anything, by the time you get to the New Testament, it's way more anthivid."],[612.46,"The Pharisees didn't even completely accept the validity of Herod's temple because it was Herod's temple."],[620.4,"Who had who had done all of these building projects."],[623.44,"And there were huge sects."],[624.8,"I mean, uh Qumran, the Dead Sea Scrolls."],[626.98,"That's one of the most obvious, but who left the temple and wanted nothing to do with, we talked about the Sadducees last time."],[634.8,"And the corruption of the high priests."],[637.13,"They were literally the last several high priests of the temple in Jerusalem became high priest by assassinating the previous high priest."],[646.79,"What are the problems with identifying, you know, there's that passage where Christ refers to all the innocent blood between Abel and Zechariah the son of Barakiah."],[657.33,"And there's some debate as to which Zachariah that is."],[660.13,"Now, according to the tradition of the church, the teaching of the church, that's St."],[664.53,"John the Forerunner's father."],[666.51,"But part of the problem with figuring out who it is, is there are at least three people named Zachariah who were murdered in the temple."],[673.35,"Oh, wow. Who that could be in the first century."],[680.07,"So that's the kind of stuff that was going on."],[682.71,"And so Allah just had nothing to do with it."],[686.09,"And of course, Christ talks about destroying the temple rebuilding, referring to his own body."],[692.66,"Where Christ sort of supersedes the temple."],[695.54,"And of course, then that culminates at the end of the book of Revelation in the new heavens and new earth."],[701.22,"St. John explicitly says, there is no temple."],[705.14,"Because God and the Lamb dwell with them."],[708.68,"There's no need for a temple structure now."],[712.92,"So talking about a heavenly temple doesn't work once we understand how temple works."],[721.26,"That temple, again, is one of these provisional things."],[727.56,"That does have a reality to it."],[729.64,"Now the tabernacle is there in the Torah."],[733.74,"And so that's what we're we're dealing with."],[737.26,"We're dealing with here."],[738.54,"The tabernacle is the thing that God intended."],[741.59,"And so, if the question is, did God intend for it always to be the tabernacle?"],[747.11,"The answer is yes."],[749.83,"Because there's actually a group of people who lived a clan within within uh uh Israel who until the exile never settled."],[764.35,"They lived in tents on the land that was their land grant in the Torah."],[769.23,"And they were praised for that."],[772.64,"They never put down roots."],[774.48,"And so there was um there was a principle there built into the Torah of and this is you know, the feast of booths, the feast of tabernacles, where you sort of uproot yourself and and at the Passover, you dress and eat as if you're about to go on a journey."],[792.0,"This idea that even when they got into the land, th this world isn't our final home."],[799.41,"We're on our way somewhere else"],[802.15,"We can't become too settled and fall too much in love with this world and the things of this world."],[806.95,"And so God living in a tent was part of that too."],[810.97,"That was mobile."],[813.13,"Um so uh this is why now we're gonna talk all about the tabernacle and not the tent."],[821.21,"And um what you have here in verse 2 is the idea that there is a tabernacle, a true tabernacle, which the Lord erected and not man."],[834.17,"So, what is this referring to?"],[836.81,"Well, this is referring to the symbolism of what was going on with the tabernacle in the first place."],[841.93,"This is gonna get expanded on in the next paragraph. but um, of what was going on in the with the tabernacle in the first place"],[849.25,"Because when you read ancient Near Eastern texts from this period about where the gods live."],[857.17,"Gods live on mountains."],[860.06,"We're all probably familiar with that."],[861.36,"Mount Olympus. Mountains of own, etc. etc."],[864.12,"Gods all live up on a mountain."],[865.64,"They also live in gardens."],[867.08,"And this is especially prominent in the ancient Near East because you got a lot of desert."],[872.65,"So if desert is what you're stuck with, well, the gods must live in this sort of bountiful garden."],[878.66,"On top of a mountain."],[880.5,"Uh, in this sort of inacceptable place."],[882.74,"But in those places, they lived in these sort of pavil.ion tents"],[890.32,"When we see these scenes play out, sort of the divine council, the assembly of the gods, the assembly of the divine beings, happens inside like a big pavilion tent where they gather and meet."],[903.97,"So St. Paul is sort of reinvoking that idea."],[909.83,"So Christ isn't serving in a tabernacle, much less a temple here on this earth, the way the Levitical priesthood did."],[920.55,"He's in the presence of God."],[922.15,"He's in the place where God dwells."],[924.57,"He's in the divine assembly there."],[927.38,"That's where his high priestly service is."],[930.44,"Is that the throne of God?"],[933.48,"And now this is going to get developed a little more."],[937.08,"Verse 3. For every high priest is appointed to offer both gifts and sacrifices."],[944.92,"Therefore, it is necessary that this one also have something to offer."],[952.04,"So, high priests offer gifts and sacrifice."],[956.28,"What's the difference?"],[957.0,"Well, we've talked before about how worship in general was understood in the ancient world within the context of hospitality."],[965.66,"The ideas of hospitality and forging this communion between each other in the community and the community and their God."],[977.43,"And hospitality primarily takes the form of food."],[985.45,"Sharing a meal."],[986.58,"And that's where sacrifice comes in."],[988.74,"Sacrifices are meals."],[990.9,"The other thing is gifts."],[994.92,"Someone comes, you welcome someone in your home, you give them a gift."],[997.48,"Or someone invites you into their home, into their home, you give them a gift."],[1002.74,"And so this is another another category."],[1006.42,"It's not really separate."],[1007.88,"But another aspect."],[1009.98,"And so this is where the idea like of a sacrifice of praise or that kind of thing comes from."],[1014.46,"It's a gift"],[1017.19,"Um this is an old-timey sense of gift."],[1020.25,"But if you cave this one invited to your house, you came and you wrote them a poem that you read to them."],[1025.43,"Or saying that was a that could be seen as a gift."],[1029.21,"This is something I'm doing."],[1030.97,"So uh basically, those are the categories to uh cover worship."],[1036.65,"That's why you have a high priest."],[1039.27,"Is is this ritual worship."],[1042.72,"Not to be the boss, not to whatever else."],[1045.94,"Not to own all the land like the high priests in the first century did."],[1050.98,"Um and so that means if if Christ now is the high priest, then he's offering something."],[1060.27,"If that we're calling him a high priest, it's because of that."],[1063.87,"Verse 4, for if he were on earth, he would not be a priest."],[1068.51,"Since there are priests who offer the gifts according to the law, okay, so this is building off of that."],[1078.03,"Remember, Grice isn't part of the Levitical Priesthood."],[1083.6,"So if he were here serving on earth, so if he were a priest at the temple in Jerusalem."],[1093.53,"Well, if he were in Jerusalem, he would not be in the temple offering sacrifices because he's not a biblical priesthood."],[1099.57,"So, yeah, yeah"],[1099.85,"There are Levitical priests who do that in that temple."],[1103.37,"Yeah, yeah. Um, because there are priests who do that under the Torah."],[1112.42,"According to the Torah."],[1113.94,"Verse 5, who serve the copy and shadow of the heavenly things, as Moses was divinely instructed when he was about to make the tabernacle, for he said, See that you make all things according to the pattern shown you on the mountain."],[1135.78,"And uh that's from uh Exodus."],[1145.58,"So what this is saying is that when Moses went up on the mountain, Mount Sinai, Mount Sinai had become the mountain of God."],[1160.78,"Mount Sinai was being the mountain of God, the mountain of assembly, armoeid in Hebrew, at that point."],[1167.58,"So when Moses went up there, we already talked about the law given through angels part."],[1174.16,"That he goes up there and he enters into right"],[1178.81,"God is enthroned there."],[1181.11,"Until he speaks to God face to face there."],[1183.67,"The angels are there, he enters into this assembly, into God as king reigning in his council, which means he enters into this tent of assembly, this tent of meeting."],[1194.93,"This tabernacle, this place where God lives."],[1197.73,"And so when he comes down the mountain, and the tabernacle is constructed, it's constructed as uh a replica, essentially, of where he was and what he saw."],[1214.81,"And so that's why there are cherubim and angelic beings woven into the right, why there's all this imagery."],[1225.05,"And on the the Ark of the Covenant itself, there are cherubim."],[1228.86,"Carved, three-dimensional c,overed in gold."],[1232.42,"Sorry, iconoclass. And there's the most holy place."],[1238.22,"Where the throne, where the throne of God is."],[1242.48,"And then there's the exterior, where the angelic beings are."],[1246.17,"And this, of course, is the same pattern that we still use in Orthodox churches."],[1249.71,"Now, in addition to angelic beings, we have the icons of the saints, who are also part of that heavenly council of God."],[1258.03,"And we have the altar that is his footstool."],[1260.45,"And we have so it's the same structure."],[1262.45,"We're just much further in as we talked about last time."],[1266.01,"Um so the main reason this is important is that this adds an important to new important nuance. to what we're saying about the relationship between the old covenant and the new covenant"],[1279.05,"So it's not just that the old covenant was sort of this preliminary partial first thing that was kind of good, but now we have this that's better."],[1291.79,"And it replaces that."],[1294.24,"The reality that we have now actually came first."],[1300.88,"Christ came first, as we already saw in the first part of Hebrews, Christ was before Moses and before Abraham."],[1308.84,"So this actually came first, and the Torah was the beginning of that heavenly reality, that eternal reality, that spiritual reality coming into our world."],[1326.16,"And it has now arrived in our world more fully in Christ."],[1332.48,"And then that comes to fruition in the age to come."],[1336.96,"When the heavenly reality, heaven and earth are united and become one."],[1347.04,"When those two finally become one."],[1350.12,"So this is not sort of a new reality that comes into existence."],[1358.7,"But this is an eternal reality that is now entering into our human experience."],[1367.96,"First in the form of just the imagery of it, and then now in a more fulfilled sense, pointing us toward the ultimate fulfillment in the age to come."],[1379.0,"So there's multiple stages here."],[1382.12,"But the change is happening within our material world of human experience."],[1390.6,"In eternity, nothing is changing."],[1400.05,"And so that means that these Levitical priests are serving in this copy."],[1403.97,"in. This replica of what's going on heaven"],[1408.95,"But Christ as high priest is in the real thing."],[1413.71,"Again, supersedes. So not only does his priesthood supersede the Levitical priesthood, but what the place where he conducts his priesthood, not only supersedes it, but was the basis, it's a copy."],[1426.19,"It's a xerox."],[1427.31,"It's the great value brand."],[1430.48,"Of uh Tabernacle."],[1437.28,"Verse 6, but now he has obtained a more excellent ministry."],[1441.04,"And as much as he is also mediator of a better covenant, which was established on better promises."],[1446.88,"And that's introducing what he's about to talk about"],[1452.44,"And he's not saying Christ has made has obtained a more excellent ministry than Christ had before."],[1461.4,"He's saying Christ has obtained a more excellent ministry than the ministry of those Levitical priests."],[1477.58,"Because as we talked about last time, the Torah never promised eternal life for keeping the commandments."],[1487.07,"So those aren't the promises it was based on."],[1491.39,"And so that's part of what makes the new covenant better."],[1494.83,"So verse 7."],[1497.63,"For if the first covenant had been faultless, then no place would have been sought for a second."],[1504.83,"So if the Torah had promised those things, then you wouldn't need a right."],[1514.4,"If you could have gotten eternal life by not committing adultery and cleansing your mildew properly, then that would have, that would have been sufficient."],[1524.56,"There would have been no need for anything else."],[1526.72,"Now, note, because this is important theologically."],[1531.98,"A lot of our Protestant friends will say that the problem with the Torah."],[1534.22,"will say, They that if you did keep the Torah you would earn eternal life."],[1542.4,"So they will argue that the problem with the Torah is not with the Torah itself, but that you can't keep it because we're sinful, because we're depraved."],[1552.8,"We can't do it."],[1554.16,"If we could do it, then we would."],[1556.82,"And in fact, part and parcel of that theological system is that that's how Christ gives us eternal life, is that Christ earned it by keeping the Torah."],[1567.38,"But notice that's not what St."],[1569.22,"Paul is saying here."],[1572.26,"St. Paul is saying that the old covenant, the Torah, was founded on inferior promises."],[1578.5,"So the problem that St"],[1581.58,"Paul is pointing to here that required the new covenant is not that the people couldn't keep it."],[1589.26,"It's that keeping it didn't accomplish what Christ accomplishes."],[1596.3,"So you can't make that kind of theological system jibe with what Hebrews is saying right here."],[1605.82,"Because Hebrews is saying the Torah was never about that."],[1609.02,"Very clearly. Verse 8."],[1614.46,"Now we get a block quote."],[1617.38,"It's a long quote from St."],[1620.3,"Paul. Because finding fault with them, he says, Behold, the days are coming, says the Lord, when I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel, and with the house of Judah, not according to the covenant that I made with their fathers in the day when I took them by the hand to lead them out of the land of Egypt, because they did not continue in my covenant, and I disregarded them, says the Lord."],[1644.58,"For this is the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel after those days, says the Lord."],[1650.02,"I will put my laws in their mind and write them on their hearts, and I will be their God, and they shall be my people."],[1656.5,"None of them shall teach his neighbor, and none his brothers, saying, Know the Lord, for all shall know me, from the least of them to the greatest of them."],[1665.46,"For I will be merciful to their unrighteousness, and their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more."],[1673.18,"So this is from Jeremiah 31, uh in the uh in the Hebrew chapter numbering."],[1683.02,"Um but and then Jeremiah 31 is famous as being sort of the new covenant passage."],[1690.42,"Um notice in the block quote, though, what is the difference, according to Yahweh, the God of Israel, between the old covenant and the new covenant."],[1706.42,"The old covenant, they didn't keep it, and so God abandoned them."],[1717.04,"Not they weren't able to."],[1721.89,"The new covenant in this quote, the difference with the new covenant is that the new covenant is something God is going to do."],[1731.35,"God is going to do."],[1732.47,"Now, does he say I'm going to save every one of them?"],[1737.19,"No. He says they're all going to know me."],[1741.35,"They're all going to know me."],[1744.75,"Um, from the least to the greatest."],[1746.87,"So that no one will have to teach his neighbor how to know God, because they will all know God."],[1753.03,"And they will all know his laws, his commandments."],[1756.35,"So laws and commandments don't get done away with in the new covenant."],[1761.99,"And that he will offer forgiveness."],[1768.09,"He'll offer forgiveness."],[1770.33,"So um, what's the contrast here?"],[1774.97,"The contrast is between people didn't know God."],[1780.71,"And that created this rupture."],[1783.59,"New covenant came to an end."],[1786.09,"Yeah, the old covenant, the old covenant came to an end because there was this rupture."],[1790.67,"What does it mean that people couldn't know God?"],[1793.32,"Well, we don't know all psychologically, but as we talked about last time."],[1799.73,"Very few people could go into the tabernacle or the temple, at least very far in."],[1805.11,"There was this disconnect."],[1808.25,"There was this disconnect."],[1809.29,"And that was connected to possessing the Holy Spirit."],[1811.77,"You can't separate those."],[1812.99,"Because who received the Holy Spirit in the Old Testament?"],[1816.67,"Prophets, judges. Moses, the king."],[1822.17,"But not everybody."],[1823.79,"So there were certain people who knew the Lord."],[1826.11,"There were certain people who were empowered."],[1827.95,"There were certain people who are in his presence."],[1830.09,"And then there were the common people."],[1832.41,"Whereas the new covenant, everyone, everyone knows the Lord."],[1836.89,"Now, does everyone knowing the Lord automatically produce salvation for them?"],[1841.59,"Well, were all the kings of Israel who received the Holy Spirit saints?"],[1848.15,"I mean, Saul certainly wasn't."],[1850.16,"Like the Holy Spirit leaves him."],[1852.26,"And David has David prays and pleads after his sin that the Lord won't take the Holy Spirit from him."],[1860.31,"Wouldn't depart from him."],[1862.47,"And the Holy Spirit departed from the temple completely."],[1867.61,"Because of the sins of the people."],[1869.47,"That's what he's talking about, disregarding them."],[1871.96,"He had to leave."],[1874.06,"Because of because of their sins."],[1876.4,"So just God's presence doesn't automatically produce salvation."],[1881.88,"In fact, it ramps up the accountability."],[1886.34,"It ramps up the accountability."],[1887.94,"If you know God, if if God's law is written in your heart and you don't have to go study it, you have a lot fewer excuses."],[1895.28,"In terms of not keeping it correctly."],[1897.93,"Your average farmer out somewhere in the hinterlands of Israel, you know, might not have known exactly how he was supposed to get rid of Mildew."],[1908.91,"might have He he was illiterate."],[1910.49,"He didn't have access to the temple on a regular basis."],[1913.43,"Um, but if we all know God, and we all know what he commands and what he needs to do, now we're very much accountable."],[1920.87,"Now we're very much accountable"],[1927.29,"But there's also this new level of forgiveness available."],[1933.86,"Where we're not just in the Torah managing the sins of the people so God can stay."],[1940.44,"But we're talking about actual forgiveness and reconciliation so that we can continue to know God, not just have him in a building nearby where we can go visit and offer a sacrifice once a year."],[1952.5,"But that we can know him."],[1955.7,"If the forgiveness is there and and he will not remember their lawless deeds and so on and so forth. um, it doesn't say that some people are exempt from that"],[1970.14,"It sounds like he's forgiving everybody everything."],[1974.94,"Well, we'll see."],[1978.78,"But I mean, you have to take it to the context of Hebrews."],[1980.86,"He's already talked about people who aren't."],[1983.88,"So this is talking about the people who this isn't saying that right, the new covenant isn't with every human on earth."],[1992.26,"Okay. It's potentially with every human on earth."],[1995.07,"But this is talking about people who are party to that."],[1997.19,"Just like the old covenant, there are people who are party to it and people who weren't."],[2001.41,"So if you're party to the new covenant, then yes, you have the forgiveness of your sins and you have the knowledge of God."],[2006.13,"of But that's not to say that any that is irrevocable."],[2006.37,"You can leave the covenant"],[2018.87,"Is talks about this applying to Judah and Israel."],[2022.55,"Yes. Um I I know the church is the new Israel and all that, but it sounds like it's talking about Judah and Israel."],[2034.05,"We gotta go back to Romans eleven."],[2036.31,"Okay. Because this is one of many promises of the prophets that about Judah and Israel, or Judah and Ephraim, as Israel is often called."],[2047.92,"And the problem with it being about Judah and Israel is Israel's gone."],[2049.84,"Long gone Sorry, British people who think you're the lost tribes."],[2061.78,"And so what St."],[2063.54,"Paul does in Romans 11, not to go through all that again, but just refresher."],[2068.66,"He uses this phrase about the fullness of the Gentiles."],[2073.48,"He says that that uh Judah has been hardened."],[2078.58,"Because remember, Judah's just part."],[2080.1,"It's one and a half tribes."],[2081.78,"There's some Benjamites in there and some um has been hardened until the fullness of the Gentiles has come in."],[2091.43,"And people. have all kinds, especially the Puritans, have all kinds of interesting theories on what that means"],[2096.91,"But if you go by the Bible, there's one other place where that phrase the fullness of the Gentiles occurs, and that's back in the book of Genesis, when uh Jacob is blessing Joseph's sons, Manasseh and Ephraim."],[2112.75,"Ephraim, who the northern king was named after."],[2115.39,"And he does one of those things that's so typical in Genesis where Ephraim is actually the younger of the two, but he crosses his hands, his right and left hands, when he's gonna do the blessing, and gives the bigger blessing to the younger son, Ephraim."],[2132.91,"But the prophecy he gives of the blessing he gives is that Ephraim's descendants will become the fullness of the Gentiles."],[2137.07,"'s. That the other place where the phrase occurs"],[2143.96,"So what St."],[2144.92,"Paul does picking up on that is say, well, wait a second."],[2150.89,"His descendants are become the fullness of the Gentiles."],[2153.93,"All of these prophecies say these ten tribes are going to come back."],[2157.69,"They were scattered out there among the nations, among the Gentiles, and interbreeding."],[2163.29,"They are the Gentiles."],[2164.49,"So for them to come back, they have to come back from the Gentiles."],[2168.17,"And so that's what St."],[2169.29,"Paul says means when he says that the Jews have been has been hardened for a time until the fullness of the Gentiles comes in, and then, as a result, all Israel will be saved, all 12 tribes."],[2180.49,"Because those other ten tribes are being reconstituted from the nations"],[2188.54,"So it's not that the church is the new Israel, it's that the church is Israel."],[2195.11,"Um, just full stop."],[2197.19,"It's Israel. Um, yeah."],[2200.79,"Yeah, so that's that's how Saint Paul understands there in Romans 9 through 11, how he understands why now the gospel is going out to all the nations."],[2214.39,"Because we have to reconstitute the people of God, and you have to go find them where they went, and that's out there, intermarried into. the nations"],[2225.15,"I don't think it really implies that that they're biological descendants."],[2232.91,"No, no, because they didn't have a concept of DNA like that."],[2236.85,"Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah."],[2238.37,"But they become part of Israel again the same way ritually."],[2242.14,"They become part of the covenant through baptism, through receiving the Eucharist."],[2247.82,"Yeah, they become part of the people of God."],[2256.86,"Verse 13, in that he says, a new covenant, he has made the first obsolete."],[2263.82,"Now, what is becoming obsolete and growing old is ready to vanish away."],[2268.9,"The older I get, I get, the more attacked I feel by that verse."],[2272.66,"But um, but so he's making the point."],[2280.63,"If God is all the way back in Jeremiah, so back when the first temple was destroyed."],[2286.49,"All the way back then he's promising a new covenant."],[2289.67,"Well, that means that first one is in some sense obsolete."],[2296.17,"It's not, oh, we're gonna give this one another go, we're gonna try harder this time."],[2300.82,"Because it was never intended for that."],[2303.8,"It was never intended for that."],[2305.56,"Obsolete means it served its purpose."],[2309.46,"And now this higher purpose, it can't serve."],[2312.1,"And so we need the new."],[2314.76,"The better to fulfill the greater function."],[2319.72,"And so because it's obsolete, growing old."],[2325.89,"And that, of course, is how Christianity has historically understood why the temple gets destroyed and why then Jerusalem gets destroyed after the Bar Kokhva rebellion."],[2337.14,"That's it vanishing away."],[2339.7,"It's not needed anymore."],[2344.98,"And that that becomes that becomes for the for the church fathers who we see interacting with rabbinic Judaism, that becomes their primary argument."],[2353.3,"You know, St"],[2359.9,"John Chrysostom says, you know, Judaism by that time in the fourth, late fourth, early fifth century, no longer had a temple, no longer had a priesthood, no longer had sacrifices."],[2374.3,"That's a lot of the Torah."],[2378.11,"That's a massive portion of the Torah that's gone."],[2382.61,"And so that was the sign that look, it's obsolete."],[2387.51,"That that's over."],[2389.19,"And he says, of course, that the church has temple, priesthood, sacrifice."],[2396.63,"But you find that everywhere you find that in uh St"],[2398.03,"That Judaism no longer had."],[2404.27,"Justin Martyr, his dialogue with Trifo, uh St."],[2408.51,"Justin the Philosopher."],[2410.03,"Um, all the way through, this is a constant."],[2413.01,"Look, that's that stuff's gone because right, it's no longer necessary."],[2419.06,"This is a sign that what Christians are saying is true."],[2423.78,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2431.14,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2436.5,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":34.0,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Melchizedek vs Levitical priesthood","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Last time: <b>Hebrews 7</b> - back to <b>Melchizedek</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Connected <b>Genesis</b> and <b>Psalm 110</b> to <b>Christ's priesthood</b>; contrasted with <b>Levitical</b> priesthood</li><li class=\"d1\">Levitical = particular priesthood for a <b>particular window</b></li><li class=\"d1\">did not precede <b>Moses</b> - no Levitical priesthood until the <b>Torah</b></li><li class=\"d1\">it has an <b>end</b>; Melchizedek's <b>supersedes</b> it</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Supersede\" does not mean old thing out, new thing in</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ does what it did <b>fully and completely</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Levitical did it <b>limitedly and provisionally</b> - fullness here, no need for the partial</li></ul>"},{"t":164.85,"lv":3,"h":"From priesthood to liturgy","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Paul now moves from the priest to the <b>liturgical aspect</b> - what the priest <b>does</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>tabernacle</b> structure and <b>sacrificial ritual</b></li><li class=\"d0\">First chapters: Christ as <b>Son of God, Messiah, king, Lord</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Now: what Christ does <i>qua</i> <b>high priest</b></li></ul>"},{"t":245.07,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 8:1-2 - The main point","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"This is the <b>main point</b>\" - not of the whole epistle, <b>one of them</b></li><li class=\"d0\">After Melchizedek and Levitical priesthood: \"we have such a <b>high priest</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">seated at the right hand - <b>Psalm 110:1</b>, where he started</li><li class=\"d1\">\"Yahweh said to my Lord, sit at my right hand…\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Minister of the sanctuary</b> = the <b>holy place</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the place where <b>God is</b> is the holy place</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>True tabernacle</b>, \"which the Lord erected, and not man\"</li></ul>"},{"t":349.48,"lv":3,"h":"Tabernacle, not temple","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">All through here: the <b>tabernacle</b>, not the temple</li><li class=\"d0\">Apostles, even <b>Paul</b>, prayed at the Jerusalem temple - but temple is an <b>ambivalent category</b> in Scripture</li><li class=\"d0\">Our assumptions shaped by <b>evangelicalism</b>, <b>Scofield Reference Bible</b>, <b>dispensationalism</b></li><li class=\"d1\">rebuilt temple as eschatological <b>good thing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">church fathers: a very <b>bad</b> thing</li><li class=\"d0\">Torah never says \"build a <b>permanent temple</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Tabernacle details given <b>twice</b> in Exodus - \"a real bore\"</li><li class=\"d1\">instructions, then \"and so they built it\" - <b>exactly as commanded</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no such narrative for the temple</li></ul>"},{"t":439.61,"lv":3,"h":"Solomon's temple and God's answer","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>David</b> wants to build it - God says <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Solomon</b> builds it; dedication prayer talks like the <b>pagans</b> about temples</li><li class=\"d1\">\"grant whatever we pray toward this temple\", honor vows and sacrifices</li><li class=\"d0\">God's response: he <b>doesn't live in buildings made by human hands</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not constrained or tied to it</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I will be present with you here, but <b>not on human terms</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">He leaves; <b>Nebuchadnezzar</b> destroys it</li><li class=\"d1\">in <b>Jeremiah</b> everyone says Babylon can't take Jerusalem - \"his temple\"</li></ul>"},{"t":601.47,"lv":3,"h":"The temple by the first century","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">How people looked at the temple vs how <b>God</b> did - very different</li><li class=\"d0\">By the NT, <b>more ambivalent</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Pharisees didn't fully accept <b>Herod's</b> temple</li><li class=\"d1\">sects left it: <b>Qumran</b>, Dead Sea Scrolls</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Corruption</b>: last several high priests got in by <b>assassinating</b> the previous one</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Zechariah</b> son of Barakiah (innocent blood, Abel to Zechariah)</li><li class=\"d1\">church tradition: <b>John the Forerunner's father</b></li><li class=\"d1\">problem: at least <b>three Zechariahs</b> murdered in the temple</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ: destroy this temple → his own <b>body</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Revelation</b>: new heavens and earth - \"there is <b>no temple</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God and the Lamb dwell with them</li><li class=\"d1\">\"heavenly temple\" doesn't work - temple is <b>provisional</b></li></ul>"},{"t":729.64,"lv":3,"h":"The tabernacle was always God's intent","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Tabernacle is what <b>God intended</b> - always</li><li class=\"d0\">A clan in Israel <b>never settled</b> until the exile</li><li class=\"d1\">lived in <b>tents</b> on their land grant; <b>praised</b> for it</li><li class=\"d0\">Built into Torah: <b>feast of booths/tabernacles</b>; at <b>Passover</b> eat dressed for a journey</li><li class=\"d1\">\"this world isn't our <b>final home</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">don't fall in love with the world</li><li class=\"d0\">God living in a <b>tent</b> = mobile, part of that</li></ul>"},{"t":821.21,"lv":3,"h":"The true tabernacle: gods' pavilion tents","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Verse 2: a <b>true tabernacle</b> the Lord erected - what was the tabernacle symbolizing?</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient Near East: gods live on <b>mountains</b> (Olympus) and in <b>gardens</b></li><li class=\"d1\">desert region → gods in bountiful garden on a mountain</li><li class=\"d1\">and in <b>pavilion tents</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Divine council</b> meets inside a big pavilion tent</li><li class=\"d0\">Paul reinvokes this</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ not serving in a tabernacle or temple on earth</li><li class=\"d1\">he's in <b>God's presence</b>, the <b>divine assembly</b>, at the <b>throne of God</b></li></ul>"},{"t":937.08,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 8:3 - Gifts and sacrifices","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Every high priest appointed to offer <b>gifts and sacrifices</b> → Christ must have something to offer</li><li class=\"d0\">Difference? Worship understood as <b>hospitality</b></li><li class=\"d1\">forging <b>communion</b> in community and with God</li><li class=\"d1\">hospitality = <b>food</b>; <b>sacrifices are meals</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Gifts</b>: you bring a gift when welcomed into a home</li><li class=\"d1\">\"sacrifice of <b>praise</b>\" comes from here</li><li class=\"d1\">old-timey sense: a <b>poem</b> read to your host</li><li class=\"d0\">High priest = <b>ritual worship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not to be the boss or <b>own the land</b> like first-century high priests</li></ul>"},{"t":1063.87,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 8:4-5 - Copy and shadow","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Verse 4: if on earth, he <b>wouldn't be a priest</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Christ isn't <b>Levitical</b>; temple priests already serve \"according to the law\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Verse 5: they serve the <b>copy and shadow</b> of heavenly things</li><li class=\"d1\">\"See that you make all things according to the <b>pattern shown you on the mountain</b>\" - <b>Exodus</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1145.58,"lv":3,"h":"Moses on the mountain","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sinai</b> became the <b>mountain of assembly</b> (<i>har moed</i>)</li><li class=\"d0\">Moses enters God's <b>council</b>, speaks face to face; angels there</li><li class=\"d1\">enters the <b>tent of meeting</b> where God lives</li><li class=\"d0\">Tabernacle built as a <b>replica</b> of what he saw</li><li class=\"d1\">hence <b>cherubim</b> woven in; carved cherubim on the <b>Ark</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Most holy place</b> = God's throne; exterior = angelic beings</li><li class=\"d0\">Same pattern in <b>Orthodox churches</b></li><li class=\"d1\">plus <b>icons of the saints</b> in the council, <b>altar</b> as footstool</li><li class=\"d1\">\"same structure - we're just much further in\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1266.01,"lv":3,"h":"The heavenly reality came first","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Nuance on <b>old vs new covenant</b>: not just a partial first thing, replaced by something better</li><li class=\"d0\">The reality we now have <b>came first</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ was before <b>Moses and Abraham</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Torah</b> = beginning of heavenly, eternal reality entering our world</li><li class=\"d1\">more fully in <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">fruition in the <b>age to come</b>: heaven and earth become one</li><li class=\"d0\">Change is in our <b>human experience</b>; in eternity <b>nothing changes</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Levitical priests serve a <b>replica</b>; Christ in the <b>real thing</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"a <b>Xerox</b>... the great value brand\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1437.28,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 8:6 - A more excellent ministry","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mediator of a better covenant</b>, on <b>better promises</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Better than the <b>Levitical priests'</b> ministry - not better than before</li><li class=\"d0\">Torah <b>never promised eternal life</b> for keeping the commandments</li><li class=\"d1\">so not those promises - part of what makes the new covenant better</li></ul>"},{"t":1497.63,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 8:7 - If the first were faultless","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"If the first covenant had been <b>faultless</b>, no place sought for a second\"</li><li class=\"d0\">If Torah promised eternal life → no need for another</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not committing adultery and cleansing your <b>mildew</b> properly\" would suffice</li></ul>"},{"t":1526.72,"lv":3,"h":"The Protestant reading","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Protestant view: keep the Torah → <b>earn eternal life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">problem is we can't - <b>depravity</b></li><li class=\"d1\">so Christ <b>earned it by keeping the Torah</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Not what Paul says: old covenant founded on <b>inferior promises</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Problem isn't people <b>couldn't keep it</b></li><li class=\"d1\">keeping it <b>didn't accomplish</b> what Christ accomplishes</li><li class=\"d0\">That system doesn't \"<b>jibe</b>\" with Hebrews - Torah was never about that</li></ul>"},{"t":1609.02,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 8:8-12 - The Jeremiah 31 quote","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Long block quote: <b>Jeremiah 31</b> (Hebrew numbering) - the <b>new covenant</b> passage</li><li class=\"d1\">\"not according to the covenant... when I took them by the hand\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>I will put my laws in their mind</b> and write them on their hearts\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"all shall <b>know me</b>, from the least to the greatest\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"I will be merciful... sins I will <b>remember no more</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1690.42,"lv":3,"h":"The difference: God will do it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Old covenant: they didn't keep it → God <b>abandoned</b> them - not that they weren't <b>able</b></li><li class=\"d0\">New covenant: something <b>God is going to do</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not \"I'll save every one\" - they'll all <b>know me</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no one teaches neighbor to know God</li><li class=\"d1\">laws and commandments <b>not done away</b></li><li class=\"d1\">and he'll offer <b>forgiveness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Old covenant ended through a <b>rupture</b>: few could go <b>far into the tabernacle</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Tied to the <b>Holy Spirit</b></li><li class=\"d1\">OT: <b>prophets, judges, Moses, the king</b> - not everybody</li><li class=\"d1\">new covenant: <b>everyone</b> knows the Lord</li></ul>"},{"t":1836.89,"lv":3,"h":"Knowing God raises accountability","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Does everyone knowing the Lord automatically save? <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Saul</b> received the Spirit - it left him</li><li class=\"d1\">David prays the Spirit not be taken</li><li class=\"d1\">Spirit <b>departed from the temple</b> over the people's sins</li><li class=\"d0\">God's presence → <b>more accountability</b></li><li class=\"d1\">law written on heart, \"a lot fewer <b>excuses</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">illiterate farmer might not know how to deal with <b>mildew</b>; now \"very much accountable\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1927.29,"lv":3,"h":"Real forgiveness; who is party to it","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">New level of <b>forgiveness</b> - not just <b>managing sins</b> so God can stay</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Reconciliation</b>: know him, not a building to visit once a year</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: sounds like forgiving everybody everything?</li><li class=\"d1\">in context of Hebrews: those <b>party to the covenant</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>potentially</b> every human; not automatic</li><li class=\"d1\">you can <b>leave the covenant</b> - not irrevocable</li></ul>"},{"t":2018.87,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: Judah and Israel - Romans 11","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: prophecy says <b>Judah and Israel</b> - isn't the church the new Israel?</li><li class=\"d0\">Many prophecies: Judah and <b>Ephraim</b>; but northern <b>Israel is gone</b> (\"sorry, British people\")</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans 11</b>: Judah <b>hardened</b> until the <b>fullness of the Gentiles</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Judah = one and a half tribes (Benjamites)</li><li class=\"d1\">Puritans' theories</li><li class=\"d0\">Only other use: <b>Genesis</b> - Jacob blessing <b>Manasseh and Ephraim</b></li><li class=\"d1\">crossed hands, bigger blessing to the younger <b>Ephraim</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Ephraim's descendants = fullness of the Gentiles</li><li class=\"d0\">Ten tribes scattered among nations, <b>intermarried</b> → \"they <b>are</b> the Gentiles\"</li><li class=\"d1\">all Israel saved = <b>all 12 tribes</b> reconstituted from the nations</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Not that the church is the new Israel - the church <b>is Israel</b>. Full stop.\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Romans 9-11</b>: why the gospel goes to all nations</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: biological descendants? <b>No</b> - no concept of DNA</li><li class=\"d1\">become Israel again by <b>baptism</b> and the <b>Eucharist</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2256.86,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrews 8:13 - Obsolete","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"In that he says a <b>new covenant</b>, he has made the first <b>obsolete</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"Growing old\" - \"I feel <b>attacked</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Promise of a new covenant back in <b>Jeremiah</b> (first temple destroyed) → first already obsolete</li><li class=\"d1\">not \"give this one another go\" - never intended for that</li><li class=\"d1\">obsolete = <b>served its purpose</b>; can't serve the higher one</li><li class=\"d0\">Church's historic reading of <b>temple destroyed</b>, Jerusalem after <b>Bar Kokhba</b> revolt: \"<b>vanishing away</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2344.98,"lv":3,"h":"The fathers' argument","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Primary argument vs <b>rabbinic Judaism</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Chrysostom</b>: no temple, priesthood, or sacrifices by late 4th-early 5th c.</li><li class=\"d1\">massive portion of the Torah gone → sign it's <b>obsolete</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the church has <b>temple, priesthood, sacrifice</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same in <b>Justin Martyr</b>, <i>Dialogue with Trypho</i></li><li class=\"d0\">Sign that what Christians say is <b>true</b></li></ul>"}]});
